WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.400
Superhero movies come out every few months&nbsp;
now, but back in 1989? "Batman" was an event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.800
A milestone. Keep watching to relive that&nbsp;
special summer, or live it for the first time.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.440
On hearing that a Batman film was on its way, fans&nbsp;
of the Dark Knight did not have their hopes up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.120
For one, the campy '60s Batman series was still&nbsp;
firmly established in the pop culture canon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.360
even as more gritty interpretations were&nbsp;
making their way to the comic book pages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.480
Unfortunately — proving that nerd rage over&nbsp;
superhero castings pre-dates social media — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.840
news that Beetlejuice himself, Michael Keaton,&nbsp;
had been cast as Batman didn't help matters.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.880
To be fair, the news of Keaton's&nbsp;
casting stunned everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.480
not just comic book fans. Even Batman executive&nbsp;
producer Michael Uslan claims to have initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.280
thought the casting news was a joke and&nbsp;
trades like Variety openly mocked the choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.080
Fans started letter-writing campaigns to&nbsp;
change the studio's mind, including petitions.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.160
"A little disappointing. Because I guess&nbsp;
if you've grown up watching the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.680
and reading the comics and watching the TV&nbsp;
show it's a lot different than I expected."

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.920
Considering how many fans will still&nbsp;
tell you Keaton is their favorite Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.080
this kind of nerd rage may seem&nbsp;
inconceivable. But before you judge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.720
think about how the general public looked&nbsp;
at Michael Keaton in the '80s. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.600
all of Keaton's most commercially successful&nbsp;
films — like "Beetlejuice" and "Mr. Mom" – were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.040
comedies. So to understand how Batman fans&nbsp;
felt hearing Michael Keaton was playing Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.760
just imagine if an actor best known for his&nbsp;
sitcom work was cast in a superhero movie.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.880
The hype surrounding the release&nbsp;
of 1989's Batman is the stuff of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.000
legend. All you need for proof is the very&nbsp;
existence of so many online retrospectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
not necessarily about the film, but&nbsp;
specifically the phenomenon — often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.400
referred to as "Batmania" at the time —&nbsp;
created by Warner Bros' media machine.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.200
Forbes' Scott Mendelson writes about&nbsp;
being "consciously gripped in Bat-fever"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.040
before the release of Batman. For ComicsAlliance,&nbsp;
Patrick A. Reed recalls the, quote, "candy-filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.000
Keaton heads in supermarket checkout aisles"&nbsp;
and the "Batmobile toys in Happy Meals," before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.600
concluding that, quote, "the entire world had gone&nbsp;
Batty." The Washington Post remembers the hype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.920
transforming the film into a "preordained event,"&nbsp;
and as a result yielding huge financial returns.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.400
It worked: Batman raked in $43&nbsp;
million in its opening weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.240
breaking numerous ticket sales records,&nbsp;
including breaking the record for ticket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.000
sales not only on opening weekend,&nbsp;
but for its second weekend too.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.880
One of the simplest and most effective&nbsp;
marketing tools used to publicize Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.760
was the black-and-gold Bat emblem that&nbsp;
was seemingly in every shop in 1989.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.920
Whether you were at the supermarket buying&nbsp;
groceries, in a department store buying clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.680
or a fast food joint getting lunch,&nbsp;
the Batman logo was everywhere.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.440
Part of the genius of Batman's marketing onslaught&nbsp;
was realizing how instantly iconic that bat logo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.640
already was. It was not at all uncommon, for&nbsp;
example, to be met with the movie posters which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.600
didn't even include the name "Batman," but simply&nbsp;
the black-and-gold bat logo and the release date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.520
June 23rd. Paul Levitz, DC Comics' executive&nbsp;
vice president and publisher in 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.480
joked that the Bat logo shirts were so successful&nbsp;
that it caused a shortage of black cotton.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.880
The notion of using a superhero's logo&nbsp;
similarly in marketing has since become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.000
practically mandatory, but in 1989 it was a&nbsp;
novel and effective use of iconic imagery.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.600
Released the same month as Batman, the&nbsp;
late Prince's hit single "Batdance" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.600
as inescapable as any other Bat-related hype that&nbsp;
summer. At turns a chaotic dance song or a slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.600
funky groove, "Batdance" is liberally&nbsp;
peppered with samples from the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.840
including arguably the Joker's&nbsp;
most surprising exclamation,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.680
"This town needs an enema!"

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.000
The single got a lot of negative reviews from&nbsp;
critics, but it still hit the top of the Billboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.840
charts in August 1989. And the video, like the&nbsp;
song, is striking, wild, and kind of incoherent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.400
Prince appears as himself in&nbsp;
kind of a Batcave-y music studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.800
and as a character known as Gemini who&nbsp;
appears to be half-Batman and half-Joker.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.480
Along with stoking the already&nbsp;
raging fire of Bat hype,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.960
"Batdance" was a huge win for Prince. The&nbsp;
video won a Soul Train award and helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:23.040
make Prince's "Batman" soundtrack his first #1&nbsp;
album since 1985's "Around the World in a Day."

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.160
The notion that Batman is a darker&nbsp;
hero shouldn't feel like a revelation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.000
right? In the 21st century, if a&nbsp;
new Batman project is announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.960
you can expect a gravelly-voiced vigilante who&nbsp;
makes himself a friend to shadows. He broods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.480
he mourns, and he finds gargoyles&nbsp;
to perch on wherever he goes.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.800
It may surprise younger fans to know,&nbsp;
however, that before Tim Burton's Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.120
the Dark Knight's legacy was dominated&nbsp;
by the camp of the '60s Batman series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.480
Sure, the fans of the source material knew&nbsp;
Gotham's greatest champion had wider variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.120
in his stories than simply the over-the-top&nbsp;
parodies found in the television show. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.680
outside the comic book shop, most audiences&nbsp;
heard the name "Batman" and thought of Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.520
West and Burt Ward climbing up walls&nbsp;
or their ridiculous fistfights with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.400
the comic book sound effects like "POW"&nbsp;
and "BIFF" superimposed over the melee.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.920
Curing pop culture of this association was one&nbsp;
of the goals of lifelong Batman fan Michael Uslan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.160
in his decade-long crusade to get Batman made. In&nbsp;
the "Shadows of the Bat" documentary, Uslan says,

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.040
"For about 20 years, whenever anyone mentioned the&nbsp;
word 'Batman' [...] 'pow,' 'zap,' and 'wham' were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.280
always attached to the name. I wanted to detach&nbsp;
it and get back to the heart of the character."

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.120
Considering the hero's more&nbsp;
contemporary adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:43.360
it's clear 1989's "Batman" succeeded in forcing&nbsp;
the hero away from his days dancing the Batusi.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.000
Just as "Batman" forced audiences&nbsp;
to deal with a Dark Knight who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.200
far more interested in vengeance than he was puns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.560
Jack Nicholson's portrayal of the Joker made&nbsp;
the world take the cackling maniac seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.440
While he may seem cartoonish compared to&nbsp;
grittier 21st century portrayals of the Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.640
Nicholson had a take on the character that was a&nbsp;
steep departure from what most audiences expected.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.240
Sure, by 1989 Alan Moore and Brian Bolland's&nbsp;
"Batman: The Killing Joke" was making waves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.840
at the comic book shop. But the live-action Joker&nbsp;
most people knew was played by Cesar Romero in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:23.600
1960s Batman series. Not only did Romero famously&nbsp;
refuse to even shave his mustache for the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.200
but we are in no danger of being contradicted&nbsp;
when we point out that the hero of 1989's Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.840
kills more people in that film than Romero's&nbsp;
Joker does in the entire '60s series.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.440
There's no better proof that Nicholson is largely&nbsp;
responsible for the more complex directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.520
Joker has taken in the more contemporary&nbsp;
times than the complaints of the guy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.520
played him for two years. When the Associated&nbsp;
Press asked for his reaction to the 1989 film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.360
Cesar Romero called it, quote,&nbsp;
"dreary." Romero lamented,

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.880
"Nicholson, who is such a wonderful actor and&nbsp;
who has done such good work, is just so violent."

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.800
While there will probably always be naysayers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.720
the stories of comic book superheroes&nbsp;
slogged a long road toward any kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.600
legitimacy — and 1989's Batman was one&nbsp;
of the biggest steps down that road.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.760
In the "Shadows of the Bat" documentary,&nbsp;
Michael Uslan gives an example of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.280
superhero comics were viewed pre-Batman that still&nbsp;
would sound familiar to any fan of the genre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.280
While attending Indiana University&nbsp;
in the '70s, Uslan took advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.360
a program that allowed students to pitch&nbsp;
courses that they would teach themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.800
Uslan says when he applied to teach a course about&nbsp;
comic book heroes, the college dean told him comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.160
books were "cheap entertainment" and "junk" not&nbsp;
worthy of academic study. According to Uslan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.840
he responded by illustrating how the&nbsp;
origin story of Superman mirrored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.320
that of the Biblical figure Moses, and as a&nbsp;
result his course was immediately approved.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.680
"Batman's" success not only helped legitimize&nbsp;
superhero movies, but made them more attractive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.560
to actors and directors. Speaking about&nbsp;
this to the Washington Post, Andrew Farago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.880
author and curator for San Francisco's&nbsp;
Cartoon Art Museum, listed visionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.520
directors who would later attach themselves&nbsp;
to superhero franchises, aptly wondering

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.840
"Who could have imagined a world&nbsp;
with Kenneth Branagh directing Thor?"

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.440
Along with their many Easter eggs, contemporary&nbsp;
superhero movies and TV shows are known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.000
teasing fans by introducing characters&nbsp;
who — should the adaptation of the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.320
material follow closely enough — could become&nbsp;
superheroes or villains in future stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:32.080
For example, 2008's "Iron Man" hinted at James&nbsp;
Rhodes becoming the armored hero War Machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.240
but fans had to wait one more movie for it.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:36.320
"Next time, baby."

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.080
Batman gave us a very early example of this&nbsp;
kind of tease by casting Billy Dee Williams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.360
as hero District Attorney Harvey Dent. Any DC&nbsp;
Comics fan in the theater knew that Dent was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.000
destined to become the coin-tossing criminal&nbsp;
Two-Face. With this character's appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.720
the movie created a palpable sense among fans&nbsp;
that there was more to come, and did it without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.720
pandering or shouting the reference from the&nbsp;
rooftops. And while the part would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.760
be recast with Tommy Lee Jones, the franchise&nbsp;
did end up making good on its promise of adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.720
Two-Face to Batman's rogues gallery in "Batman&nbsp;
Forever." Williams, meanwhile, eventually got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.280
to play the role of Two-Face decades later&nbsp;
in the Lego Batman movie, of all places.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.160
"Batman" also created that delicious sense&nbsp;
that there was more on the way through its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.440
many absences. It was impossible not to ask&nbsp;
who would play Robin, who would play Riddler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.600
who would play Penguin when he eventually&nbsp;
showed up. Fans were casting the sequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.440
before the original was out of theaters&nbsp;
- and it was in theaters a long time.

